  "text": "\n<think>\nHere's a thinking process:\n\n1.  **Analyze User Input:**\n   - **Context:** Tuning an Intel XPU inference server.\n   - **Key Observations (repeated many times):** \n     - Stable baseline decoding\n     - Prompt-sensitive speculative acceptance\n     - Graph capture bucket sensitivity\n     - Need to preserve exact output quality while improving single-request speed\n   - **Question:** Summarize likely bottlenecks and propose an ordered plan.\n   - **Constraints:** \n     - Concise engineering analysis\n     - Concrete next steps\n     - Technical, no marketing language\n     - At least eight dense numbered paragraphs\n     - Summarize bottlenecks + ordered plan\n\n2.  **Deconstruct Key Observations & Map to Bottlenecks:**\n   - *Stable baseline decoding:* System is functional, but baseline performance is the ceiling. Suggests compute/memory bandwidth limits or kernel inefficiencies in the standard path.\n   - *Prompt-sensitive speculative acceptance:* Speculative decoding works but varies with prompt characteristics. Indicates mismatch between draft model predictions and target model acceptance criteria, or inefficient speculative scheduling/kernels.\n   - *Graph capture bucket sensitivity:* Dynamic shapes cause frequent graph recompilation/capture. Bucketing strategy is suboptimal, leading to fragmentation or suboptimal kernel selection.\n   - *Preserve exact output quality while improving single-request speed:* No approximation allowed. Must maintain deterministic/identical outputs. Rules out quantization, early exit, or probabilistic speedups. Focus on compute/memory optimization, scheduling, and graph efficiency.\n\n   Likely Bottlenecks:\n   - Memory bandwidth saturation during attention/key-value cache operations\n   - Inefficient speculative decoding pipeline (draft/target mismatch, poor scheduling)\n   - Graph capture overhead and suboptimal bucket sizing causing kernel fragmentation\n   - Compute-bound attention/FFN kernels not fully utilizing Intel XPU architecture (e.g., AMX, Xe cores)\n   - Synchronization/latency overhead in single-request path\n\n3.  **Structure the Response (8+ dense numbered paragraphs):**\n   Need to cover:\n   - Bottleneck analysis (integrated or separate)\n   - Ordered plan (concrete, technical steps)\n   - Each paragraph must be dense and technical\n   - Must be exactly or at least 8 numbered paragraphs\n   - No marketing fluff\n\n   Draft structure:\n   1. Memory bandwidth & KV cache bottleneck",
